.TH WB\-PROMPT 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-prompt \- print the prompt of a role
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR prompt <role> [<issue>] [\-\-issue <text>] [\-\-change <text>]
    [\-\-requirements <text>] [\-\-round <text>] [\-\-checkout <text>]
    [\-\-branch <text>] [\-\-issue_address <text>]
    [\-\-change_address <text>] [\-\-classed <text>]
    [\-\-classed_touched <text>] [\-\-description <text>]
    [\-\-runner <name>] [\-\-parts] [\-\-stamp]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Print the prompt of a role on standard output and nothing else: the role's text of the pinned revision with each placeholder filled from the option of its name, such as \-\-issue for {issue}, then the project's own text. The issue is the word after the role, or \-\-issue. What no option gives is taken from the repository and watchbill.yml: the checkout, the branch, the two addresses and the requirements; an option always wins.
.PP
It runs the check of wb check first and prints nothing when that fails. \-\-parts prints the path and the SHA\-256 of each part, in the form sha256sum prints, in place of the text. \-\-stamp prints, in place of the text, what the assignment is: the role, its model and effort, the runner, the version and revision, the issue, the change, the round, the branch, the checkout, the time, and each part as \-\-parts gives it.
.PP
Not with \-\-parts. \-\-runner names the runner the prompt is handed to. The breakdown needs it, and is handed out only to a runner that /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml clears for the breakdown up to the level of the repository, the line confidentiality: of watchbill.yml.
.PP
Its stamp then has the model and effort of that file, and \-\-parts adds the level and the runner. Ends with 0 when the prompt is printed, 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up. Writes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-issue <text>
The issue, for {issue}. It is also the word after the role.
.TP
.B \-\-change <text>
The change, for {change}.
.TP
.B \-\-requirements <text>
The requirements, for {requirements}.
.TP
.B \-\-round <text>
The round, for {round}.
.TP
.B \-\-checkout <text>
The checkout, for {checkout}.
.TP
.B \-\-branch <text>
The branch, for {branch}.
.TP
.B \-\-issue_address <text>
The address of the issue, for {issue_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-change_address <text>
The address of the change, for {change_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed <text>
The classed paths, for {classed}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed_touched <text>
The classed paths the change touches, for {classed_touched}.
.TP
.B \-\-description <text>
The file of the change's description, for {description}. Without it, /run/watchbill/description, where a session that wb start starts writes it.
.TP
.B \-\-runner <name>
The runner the prompt is handed to. The breakdown needs it, and is handed out only to a runner that /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml clears for the breakdown up to the level of the repository, the line confidentiality: of watchbill.yml. Its stamp then has the model and effort of that file, and \-\-parts adds the level and the runner.
.TP
.B \-\-parts
Print the path and the SHA\-256 of each part, in the form sha256sum prints, in place of the text.
.TP
.B \-\-stamp
Print, in place of the text, what the assignment is: the role, its model and effort, the runner, the version and revision, the issue, the change, the round, the branch, the checkout, the time, and each part as \-\-parts gives it. Not with \-\-parts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The prompt is printed.
.TP
.B 1
It is refused.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
